# Forbidden patterns for PHP files. Applied by .githooks/pre-commit.
# scaffold-extensions: php phtml php3 php4 php5 php7 php8
# Format: <regex><TAB><description>   (one per line; # comments ignored)
# Separator is TAB; patterns can contain literal | for ERE alternation.
# Patterns are case-SENSITIVE, scanned against the staged blob. Copy to
# your project as `.forbidden-patterns/php.txt`.
# error_log() is intentionally NOT banned - it is legitimate logging.
# die()/exit() are common control flow and live in optionalPatterns.

(^|[^A-Za-z_])var_dump[[:space:]]*\(	Remove var_dump() before committing - dumps to stdout
(^|[^A-Za-z_>])dd[[:space:]]*\(	Remove dd() (dump-and-die) before committing - it is a debug call
(^|[^A-Za-z_>])dump[[:space:]]*\(	Remove dump() (var-dumper/Laravel/Symfony) before committing
(^|[^A-Za-z_])xdebug_break[[:space:]]*\(	Remove xdebug_break() before committing

# --- Security: RCE / command-injection / TLS sinks ---
# php -l (the only PHP job the CI template runs) is a syntax check and flags
# none of these, and no other layer of the scaffold sees them.
#
# Only two rules run blocking, because only two survived being measured
# against ~20,400 real .php files (symfony, laravel/framework, WordPress,
# phpunit, doctrine/dbal, guzzle, php-src). Blanket bans on exec(), shell_exec()
# and friends, unserialize() and extract() did NOT survive: on that corpus they
# fired 482 times and essentially every hit was legitimate code - proc_open()
# with an argv array (the very "fixed argv" the advice asks for), shell_exec(
# 'stty -g') with a constant command, exec('ln -s '.escapeshellarg($t)),
# unserialize(serialize($e)) round-trips, unserialize($v, ['allowed_classes' =>
# false]) (the hardened form), extract($__data, EXTR_SKIP) in a view renderer,
# and 'extract('.$type.' from ' built as a SQL string. They are kept opt-in at
# the bottom of this file instead of shipped blocking.
#
# A third rule - "RCE sink fed straight from request data", system($_GET['cmd'])
# and friends - used to ship blocking here and no longer does. On the same
# ~20,400 files it produced exactly one hit, and that hit was a false positive
# (a guzzle security regression test); it also fired on the HARDENED
# unserialize($_COOKIE['x'], ['allowed_classes' => false]), which is the safe
# usage, and it never carried the comment guard, so linter messages, audit-log
# strings and `// unserialize($_COOKIE[...])` comments matched it. A measured
# precision of 0/1 is not a gate. A narrowed version is opt-in below.
#
# What keeps the eval() and CURLOPT rules quiet is a "nothing that opens a
# comment earlier on the line" guard: no `*`, `/` or `#` may precede the match,
# so `// eval(...)`, `# system('reboot')`, ` * @see eval()` docblock prose and a
# commented-out `// CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYPEER => false` never fire. The CURLOPT
# rule bars `'` and `"` before the match as well, so a security tool's own rule
# table (`['CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYPEER => false' => 'MITM risk']`) and
# `$pattern = "CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYHOST, 0"` stay silent; it accepts `false`,
# `FALSE` and `False`. eval() additionally requires the character immediately
# before it to be punctuation other than `.`, `>`, `:`, `$` or a quote, so
# `$c->eval(`, `Registry::eval(`, `$s.'x'.eval(`, the string `'eval()\'d code'`
# and the method declaration `public function eval(` stay silent too, and it
# requires a real argument, so a bare `eval()` written as prose - in an
# undecorated block comment, a heredoc sample or HTML copy - does not fire.
# The cost is recall: a call on a line that already contains a `/`, `*` or `#`
# earlier (`$r = 1/2; eval($c);`) is not seen, and neither is an `eval(` whose
# argument starts on the next line. That trade is deliberate - a missed call
# costs one finding, a false positive costs the gate.
# Backticks (PHP's shell-exec shorthand) are NOT covered: a regex cannot tell
# `$cmd` from a backtick in a comment or a Markdown docblock.
(^[[:space:]]*|^[^*/#]*(=>|[^A-Za-z0-9_.>:$*/#'"[:space:]]|(return|echo|print|throw|yield|<\?php)[[:space:]])[[:space:]]*)eval[[:space:]]*\([[:space:]]*[^)[:space:]]	eval() executes arbitrary code - there is no safe way to build one from input
^[^*/#'"]*CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFY(PEER|HOST)[[:space:]]*(,|=>)[[:space:]]*([Ff][Aa][Ll][Ss][Ee]|0)([^0-9]|$)	Disabling cURL TLS verification (MITM risk) - fix the cert/CA bundle instead

# Optional (opt-in) — uncomment a line to enable it:
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])print_r[[:space:]]*\(	print_r() leaks to stdout unless 2nd arg is true - opt in if your app never returns its output
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])var_export[[:space:]]*\(	var_export() leaks to stdout unless 2nd arg is true - opt in if your app never returns its output
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])(die|exit)[[:space:]]*\(	die()/exit() - often legitimate control flow; opt in if your codebase forbids it
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])(TODO|FIXME|XXX)($|[^A-Za-z0-9_])	TODO/FIXME/XXX not allowed - open a ticket instead
# (^|[^A-Za-z_.>:$])assert[[:space:]]*\(	assert(string) evaluates its argument as code on PHP 7 - off by default: assert($x instanceof Foo) is the PHPStan/Psalm type-narrowing idiom (eval() is an active rule above)
# ^[^*/#'"]*(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_>:$])(system|passthru|shell_exec|exec|popen|proc_open|pcntl_exec|eval|extract|create_function)[[:space:]]*\([^)]*\$_(GET|POST|REQUEST|COOKIE|FILES)($|[^A-Za-z0-9_])	RCE sink fed straight from request data (system($_GET['cmd'])) - off by default: it hit once in ~20,400 real files and that hit was a false positive. unserialize() and assert() are excluded here - unserialize($_COOKIE[..], ['allowed_classes' => false]) is the hardened form and assert(isset($_GET['id'])) is a plain boolean check
#
# Measured on ~20,400 real .php files, the three blanket sink bans below fire
# almost exclusively on legitimate code. Enable one only if your codebase has
# genuinely retired the construct; expect to scaffold-allow existing call sites.
# (^[[:space:]]*|^[^*/#]*(=>|[^A-Za-z0-9_.>:$*/#'"[:space:]]|(return|echo|print|throw|yield|<\?php)[[:space:]])[[:space:]]*)(system|passthru|shell_exec|exec|popen|proc_open|pcntl_exec)[[:space:]]*\(	Shell-execution sink - 135 hits on the corpus, ~all legitimate (proc_open() with an argv array, shell_exec('stty -g'), exec(...escapeshellarg($x))); enable only if you never shell out
# (^[[:space:]]*|^[^*/#]*(=>|[^A-Za-z0-9_.>:$*/#'"[:space:]]|(return|echo|print|throw|yield|<\?php)[[:space:]])[[:space:]]*)unserialize[[:space:]]*\(	unserialize() is PHP object injection when fed untrusted input - but 320 corpus hits were serialize() round-trips, cache stores and the hardened unserialize($v, ['allowed_classes' => false]); enable only if you never serialize your own data
# (^[[:space:]]*|^[^*/#]*(=>|[^A-Za-z0-9_.>:$*/#'"[:space:]]|(return|echo|print|throw|yield|<\?php)[[:space:]])[[:space:]]*)extract[[:space:]]*\(	extract() overwrites arbitrary locals - but all 9 corpus hits were view renderers doing extract($__data, EXTR_SKIP) or SQL strings; enable only if you never render PHP templates
